course: dictionary
lesson: 11568
title: Starting again with anew
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Anew is an adverb. An adverb tells you how, when or where something happens. Anew has two meanings: again, or once more, and in a new and different way, as if starting fresh. «The rain washed away our sandcastle, so we built it anew.» Here anew means we built it again, from the beginning.
explain: Look at the words around anew to choose the right meaning. «The first plan failed, so the team chose to begin anew with a fresh idea.» The clue is fresh idea, so anew means starting fresh in a new way, not just doing the same thing again.
explain: Anew is a stiff, written word. In everyday talk, people more often say again or start over. Try the swap check: if «again» or «start over» fits the sentence and keeps the same meaning, then anew is the right word, and you now know what it means.
fact: Anew is an adverb: it tells you how or when an action happens.
fact: «Anew» means again, or once more.
fact: «Anew» can also mean in a new and different way, like a fresh start.
fact: Two phrases to know are begin anew and start anew: both mean to start fresh.
fact: In everyday talk, say «again» or «start over» instead of anew.
exercise: «The rain washed away our sandcastle, so we built it anew.» What does anew mean here? | Out of sand, with no shovel | Very quickly, in a rush | Under the water | *Again, from the beginning
exercise: What is another way to say begin anew? | Shout loudly | *Start again | Stop for ever | Sleep late
exercise: «After the test, Sam decided to study anew, with new cards and a new plan.» What does anew suggest here? | Quietly and slowly | Next to the window | *In a new and different way | For the last time
test:
«Tom spilled juice on his homework, so he wrote it anew.» What does anew mean? | Under the table | Very loudly | *Again, from the beginning | Without any paper
test:
Which word is closest in meaning to anew? | Never | Slowly | *Again | Sideways
exam:
Mum says, «Let's start over and begin anew.» What do both phrases suggest? | Eating a big dinner | *Starting fresh from the beginning | Finding something lost | Stopping for ever
